implementationPlan.description:
DoD 1 — THE ROLLBACK TAG COMES FROM THE RECEIPT, NOT FROM A CONSTANT. In `apps/daemon/src/deployment/deploy-rollback.test.ts`, the existing arm at :38 asserts the run argv's last element against the module-level `digest` constant — but the harness seeded the receipt from that SAME constant, so the assertion agrees with itself and would pass even if the engine ignored the receipt. That is precisely the shape DoD 1 forbids.

Rewrite it to read the value back through the production reader:

  const kept = readDeployReceipt(store, projectId, context.request.receiptId);
  if (!kept.ok) throw new Error(kept.code);
  const run = context.docker.calls.find((args) => args[0] === "run");
  expect(run?.at(-1)).toBe(kept.receipt.imageDigest);

Assert the argv BYTE-FOR-BYTE, not just its tail: also assert the full `run` argv equals the shape `deploy-service.ts:70-71` builds (`["run", "--detach", "--name", <candidate>, "--network", <network>, <digest>]`), and that the `image inspect` argv names the same digest. Two independent argv sites naming the receipt's value is what makes "redeploys THAT image" load-bearing.

DRILL: seed the harness with a SECOND receipt carrying a different digest and roll back to it; the arm must follow the receipt rather than the constant. Then temporarily make `rollback` pass `deployImageTag(...)` instead of `selected.receipt.imageDigest` and confirm the arm reds; restore and prove it with `git diff` (tracked path).

DoD 4 — THE ATOMIC ORDER, PROVEN BY MUTATION. The existing arms assert only the END state (`upstream()` is the candidate, `app` is `STOPPED`), which a stop-then-start implementation satisfies equally. Assert the ORDER instead, from the recorded call sequence.

In `deploy-rollback.test.ts`, take indices into `context.docker.calls` and assert that the incumbent's stop happens AFTER the candidate's health has been observed healthy:

  const idx = (match) => context.docker.calls.findIndex(match);
  const health = idx((a) => a[0] === "inspect" && a.includes("{{.State.Health.Status}}"));
  const stop = idx((a) => a[0] === "stop" && a[1] === "app");
  expect(health).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0);
  expect(stop).toBeGreaterThan(health);

Also assert there is no instant at which neither serves: the candidate's `run` precedes the incumbent's `stop`, and the proxy upstream switch (the reload) also precedes it. Use `deploy-service.ts:73-75 healthArgv` as the source of the health argv shape rather than retyping the format string — a property asserted against a helper that reimplements production is forbidden by the global rail.

MUTATION DRILL, REQUIRED BY DoD 4 ("an ordering property never seen red is not evidence"): in `deploy-service.ts`, move the `["stop", lease.incumbent]` call (`:337`) to BEFORE the candidate start/health, run this arm, and QUOTE THE FAILING LINE VERBATIM in the step note. Restore, and prove restoration with `git diff -- apps/daemon/src/deployment/deploy-service.ts` (tracked, so `git diff` is valid proof here).

Do NOT hold `full-suite-gate` during this drill — it is an on-disk mutation of a shared file. Announce the UTC drill window in chat and keep it short.

DoD 6 and DoD 2's REFUSAL TRIPLE — assert the tree's refusals with code AND layer, and record the discrepancy the DoD text carries.

In `deploy-rollback.test.ts`, replace the detail-only assertion at :48 with the full shipped triple for the missing-image path:

  expect(report.receipt?.refusal).toMatchObject({
    code: "DEPLOY_BUILD_FAILED", layer: DEPLOY_ENGINE_STAMP,
    detail: "DEPLOY_ROLLBACK_IMAGE_UNAVAILABLE",
  });

IMPORT `DEPLOY_ENGINE_STAMP` rather than retyping its value, so a change to the stamp reds this arm.

STATE THE DISCREPANCY IN THE STEP NOTE AND THE COMPLETION RECORD: DoD 2 calls `DEPLOY_ROLLBACK_IMAGE_UNAVAILABLE` "the tree's code", but `deploy-service.ts:296` passes it as the DETAIL under code `DEPLOY_BUILD_FAILED` @ `DEPLOY_ENGINE_STAMP`. Do NOT promote it to a code: DoD 6 forbids renaming a dispatched code, and `apps/control-room/src/live/live-runs.ts:84` closes `DEPLOY_CODES` to four values that do not include it, so a promoted code would fail the browser decoder at :310. Introduce no new `*_LAYER` constant.

Also assert the `DEPLOY_ROLLBACK_RECEIPT_INVALID` path with code AND layer — at the engine it is the report `detail` (`deploy-service.ts:351`), and at the command seam `rollback-command.ts` throws it as a `DomainRefusal` code @ `DAEMON_COMMAND_SEAM` (rostered at `daemon-command-registry.test.ts:125`). Pin BOTH layers so a refusal moving between them cannot pass silently.
